{"name":"Canary Grass","hex":"#d0cca9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canary grass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Grass of species Phalaris canariensis, whose seeds are used as birdseed, believed to be native to Morocco and the Canary Islands, now widespread in most semitropical to cool-temperate environments.","Any grass of genus Phalaris."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canary_grass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canary grass","blocks":[{"text":"Canary grass is a plant, Phalaris canariensis, belonging to the family Poaceae. Originally a native of the Mediterranean region, it is now grown commercially in several parts of the world for birdseed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Illustration_Phalaris_canariensis0.jpg/330px-Illustration_Phalaris_canariensis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canary_grass","wikidata":"Q2086586"},{"title":"Grass","blocks":[{"text":"Grass refers to various families of plants. The three major families of grasslike plants are true grasses (Poaceae), sedges (Cyperaceae), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's arable land: rice, wheat, maize, barley, and sugar cane."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grass","wikidata":"Q643352"}],"images":[],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Carpet beige and black","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Carpet_beige_and_black.jpg/500px-Carpet_beige_and_black.jpg","ratio":0.47368421052631576,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carpet_beige_and_black.jpg","share":0.4175},{"title":"Phalaris canariensis — Flora Batava — Volume v6","image":"https://thumb.wi